{"name":"Valhalla","hex":"#2a2b41","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Valhalla","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The home of half of all warriors who died gloriously in battle as well as many of the Æsir.","A hamlet and census-designated place in the town of Mount Pleasant, Westchester County, New York, United States."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An abode of the gods or afterlife in general."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Valhalla"}],"entries":[{"title":"Valhalla","blocks":[{"text":"In Norse mythology, Valhalla is described as a majestic hall located in Asgard and presided over by the god Odin."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Walhalla_%281896%29_by_Max_Br%C3%BCckner.jpg/330px-Walhalla_%281896%29_by_Max_Br%C3%BCckner.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valhalla"},{"title":"Valhalla (steam yacht, 1892)","blocks":[{"text":"Valhalla RYS was a steam yacht, famous for her participation in the Kaiser's Trans-Atlantic Race of 1905, and the sighting of a sea serpent in the Atlantic that same year. She had several owners, most notably Joe Laycock, a trans-Atlantic racing yachtsman and Olympian, and James Lindsay, 26th Earl of Crawford, who employed her as a research vessel on three major voyages from 1902 to 1908. These resulted in the book Three Voyages of a Naturalist : Being an Account of Many Little-Known Islands in Three Oceans Visited by the 'Valhalla' R.Y.S., by M.J. Nicoll, published in 1908. During World War I she served with the Royal Navy as a part of the Eastern Mediterranean fleet's Aegean Squadron during the Gallipoli campaign. After the War she became a French-owned fruit carrier, before being wrecked off Cape St. Vincent in 1922."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/The_auxiliary_steam_yacht_Valhalla_in_1901_by_de_Simone.jpg/330px-The_auxiliary_steam_yacht_Valhalla_in_1901_by_de_Simone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valhalla_(steam_yacht%2C_1892)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/2010_Utopien_arche04.jpg/500px-2010_Utopien_arche04.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/2010_Utopien_arche04.jpg/1280px-2010_Utopien_arche04.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/2010_Utopien_arche04.jpg/500px-2010_Utopien_arche04.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2010_Utopien_arche04.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Hild%2C_Thrud_and_Hl%C3%B8kk_by_Fr%C3%B8lich.jpg/500px-Hild%2C_Thrud_and_Hl%C3%B8kk_by_Fr%C3%B8lich.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Hild%2C_Thrud_and_Hl%C3%B8kk_by_Fr%C3%B8lich.jpg/500px-Hild%2C_Thrud_and_Hl%C3%B8kk_by_Fr%C3%B8lich.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Three valkyries bearing ale in Valhalla (1895) by Lorenz Frølich","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHild%2C_Thrud_and_Hl%C3%B8kk_by_Fr%C3%B8lich.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Hundingsbane.jpg/330px-Hundingsbane.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Hundingsbane.jpg/330px-Hundingsbane.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hundingbane's Return to Valhalla (1912) by Ernest Wallcousins","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHundingsbane.jpg"},{"srcset":"